        "When Margaret Ross retired after thirty-five years of teaching biology, she expected to spend her mornings in her garden and her afternoons reading. Instead, a conversation over a fence changed her plans. Her neighbor, an eighty-year-old farmer named Tom Hale, mentioned that he would not plant his beans that spring. His knees were too weak now, he said, and his children had moved to the city years ago. The beans were a variety his grandfather had brought to the valley: tall plants with purple flowers and speckled seeds. “When I stop,” Tom said, “they stop too. Nobody else grows them.” Before she left, he pressed a glass jar of dry beans into her hands.",
